module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.Homogeneous.Ideal
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.AtPrime.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Noetherian.Defs
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.RegularLocalRing.Defs
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Ideal
import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.Module
import WeilConjectures.Mathlib.RingTheory.RegularLocalRing.Defs
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.RegularLocalGeneratorsRegularSequence

/-!
# Regularity of the graded local attraction quotient

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Lemma 13.27, printed p.263, with the
standing graded-local hypotheses preceding Lemma 13.25, printed p.262
(blueprint: lem-sfb-graded-local-attraction-regularity).

The quotient localizations invert the images of the complement of the homogeneous
maximal ideal. These are exactly the localizations at its quotient maximal ideals.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace IsRegularLocalRing

/-- Killing all negative-degree elements and the degree-zero part of a homogeneous
ideal preserves regularity at the homogeneous maximal ideal, provided the ambient
local ring and the original quotient local ring are regular.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Lemma 13.27 (Hesselink 1981, 5.6),
printed p.263; standing hypotheses on p.262
(blueprint: lem-sfb-graded-local-attraction-regularity).

Proof: Put R = A_m, n = mR, and I = aR. Localization of a quotient identifies
R/I with (A/a)_(m/a) (lem-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime-of-quotient).
The cotangent spaces m/m² and n/n², and the images of a and I in them, agree:
the inverse on a fraction x/s multiplies the class of x by the inverse residue of s.
By lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-cotangent-dimension, this space has dimension d = dim R.
Choose a homogeneous basis adapted to the image W of a, of dimension r, and lift
it to homogeneous x_1,...,x_d in m, with the first r in a.
The independent-generators theorem
(lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-quotient-independent-generators) gives I ∩ n² = nI.
Nakayama (lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators) therefore gives
I = (x_1,...,x_r)R and n = (x_1,...,x_d)R. These equalities hold before
localization: for homogeneous h, a relation sh in a homogeneous ideal with s outside
m gives s_0 h in that ideal; s_0 is a unit of A_0 (Milne 13.25--13.26).
Let J be generated by the x_i of negative degree and the x_i with i ≤ r of degree
zero. Thus J ⊆ b. Expand a negative-degree element in the x_i: every term either
contains a negative-degree x_i or has its coefficient in b and x_i in m.
Expand an element of a_0 in the first r parameters: a nonpositive-degree x_i
belongs to J, while a positive-degree x_i has a negative-degree coefficient.
Consequently b = J + mb. Since A is Noetherian, Nakayama after localization gives
bR = JR. The generators of J are a subset of a regular system of parameters of R.
Permute them to the front and apply the initial-quotient clause of
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-generators-regular-sequence, including the empty subset.
It makes R/JR regular local. Finally localization of a quotient identifies R/bR
with (A/b)_(m/b), proving the assertion. -/
theorem graded_local_attraction_regularity
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A]
    (𝒜 : ℤ → AddSubgroup A) [GradedRing 𝒜] [IsLocalRing (𝒜 0)]
    (m : Ideal A) [m.IsMaximal]
    (hm : ∀ x : A, x ∈ m ↔
      DirectSum.decompose 𝒜 x 0 ∈ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (𝒜 0))
    (a : Ideal A) (ha : a.IsHomogeneous 𝒜) (ham : a ≤ m)
    (b : Ideal A)
    (hb : b = Ideal.span ({x : A | x ∈ a ∧ x ∈ 𝒜 0} ∪
      {x : A | ∃ n : ℤ, n < 0 ∧ x ∈ 𝒜 n}))
    [IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime m)]
    [IsRegularLocalRing (Localization (m.primeCompl.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk a)))] :
    IsRegularLocalRing (Localization (m.primeCompl.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk b))) := by
  sorry

end IsRegularLocalRing
